WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.434
Hi, I'm Onyx!

00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:07.012
Altar-1 is Aikido's security model, built to run inside a customer's rack.

00:00:09.892 --> 00:00:14.486
Take GLM-5.3, delete eighty-eight of two hundred and fifty-six experts per layer,

00:00:14.486 --> 00:00:17.166
and store the rest at four bits.

00:00:19.317 --> 00:00:25.357
It starts as GLM-5.3: seven hundred and fifty-three billion parameters at full precision.

00:00:25.777 --> 00:00:29.690
A quantised build stores the routed experts at four bits: four

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.112
hundred and eighty-eight gigabytes.

00:00:31.532 --> 00:00:36.025
A prune keeps a hundred and sixty-eight per layer, calibrated on pentesting

00:00:36.025 --> 00:00:37.522
traces and multilingual text.

00:00:37.942 --> 00:00:42.037
The result: five point two four bits a parameter, not four, once

00:00:42.037 --> 00:00:44.085
scales and unquantised parts are counted.

00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:49.504
Aikido's own summary: stored weights on the left, down to three

00:00:49.504 --> 00:00:50.881
hundred and twenty-eight gigabytes.

00:00:51.301 --> 00:00:56.173
On the right, the quality argument: twenty-five of thirty-two vulnerabilities covered at

00:00:56.173 --> 00:00:58.203
full precision, twenty-three for Altar.

00:01:00.092 --> 00:01:04.027
In each expert layer, a router sends every token to a few experts.

00:01:04.447 --> 00:01:07.578
Pruning shrank the bank it picks from, to a hundred and sixty-eight.

00:01:08.704 --> 00:01:12.032
But it still picks eight: about forty billion active parameters.

00:01:12.032 --> 00:01:14.361
A token costs no less to compute.

00:01:14.781 --> 00:01:18.463
What shrinks is memory: it fits on four H200 GPUs instead

00:01:18.463 --> 00:01:19.802
of six or more.

00:01:21.092 --> 00:01:22.734
Which experts you keep matters.

00:01:22.734 --> 00:01:26.675
At a hundred and ninety-two, the best domain plan drifts far less

00:01:26.675 --> 00:01:28.317
than frequency picking or random.

00:01:29.842 --> 00:01:33.284
Altar matches its four-bit parent on thirty-one of thirty-two cases.

00:01:33.284 --> 00:01:37.760
But every case hides a known bug, so false alarms are never scored.

00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:42.875
Pruning experts costs prose roughly four times what it costs code, so

00:01:42.875 --> 00:01:45.581
a code-reading model is the right one to prune.

00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:52.671
To recap: fewer experts, four-bit weights, the same routing, nearly the same results.

00:01:53.091 --> 00:01:55.165
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:55.165 --> 00:01:55.758
I'm Onyx.

00:01:55.758 --> 00:01:56.054
Bye!
